    34	
    35	## Four things that are true, and what each licenses (r2-corrected)
    36	
    37	Each was verified against source; two of r1's four survived review intact,
    38	one was narrowed, one was overturned. What each now licenses is stated
    39	exactly.
    40	
    41	**1. The portable witness handle never carried a rowid, and nothing
    42	persists a position.** `durable_handle` (`witness.py:550-568`) emits
    43	`fact:<lineage>/<id>` and refuses to serialize unadopted/genesis positions;
    44	`handle.py`'s checkpoint machinery holds heads in memory only. **Licenses:**
    45	no persisted-position migration, no wire-format change in D1. **Does NOT
    46	license** "all rowid consumers are in libs/engine": app code compares
    47	in-memory `WitnessPosition.rowid` values to attribute a diff baseline
    48	(`apps/loops/src/loops/cli/views/fold.py:1525-1536`) — see D1 and DP-r1-07.
    49	
    50	**2. Tick commitments never contained a rowid.** `_tick_envelope`
    51	(`sqlite_store.py:158-166`) signs ten fields; `window_start` and
    52	`fact_cursor` are fact ids. The rowid appears only in how windows and edges
    53	are *resolved*. **Licenses:** signed bytes are untouched in D2 and old seals
    54	can stay verifiable — but only once every resolution site is re-keyed, on
    55	the verification path as well as construction (the r1 "three functions"
    56	blast radius was wrong; see D2), and only with a migration that assigns
    57	correct coordinates (the r1 backfill did not; see D0).
    58	
    59	**3. An order-insensitive derived-jsonl audit already exists — with set,
    60	not multiset, semantics.** `arrival_projection.audit_derived_log` (`:343`)
    61	diffs `set[bytes]` digests (`_derived_digests`, `:313`), so a *duplicated*
    62	legitimate line is invisible as an extra line. D3 wires it in and upgrades
    63	the comparison to digest **multisets** (a `Counter`), staying
    64	order-insensitive while making multiplicity part of the claim (DP-r1-08).
    65	
    66	**4. `_suffix_unindexed`'s mechanism dissolves; its capability does not.**
    67	(r1 overclaimed here — DP-r1-01.) The check does two jobs
    68	(`canonical_audit.py:383-427`): (a) corroborate that the first line past the
    69	stamped mark is genuinely unindexed — the byte-marker self-report problem,
    70	which arrival's verified anchor genuinely retires; and (b) detect a
    71	**rewound marker** — a mark sitting *below* rows the index already consumed
    72	("the marker was rewound below rows the index consumed, which no writer
    73	produces", `:400-402`). Job (b) is a real detection that must survive.
    74	Arrival-native replacement: the index holds a row with
    75	`arrival_ordinal > consumed_ordinal` — a structural query, stronger than the
    76	one-line probe it replaces. It survives as the named check `rewound` in D3.
    77	Note also that `walk_marked`'s anchor check is *self-consistency only* —
    78	"checked for self-consistency, never for chaining back to genesis"
    79	(`arrival.py:1149`) — so no L1 claim may lean on anchor validation implying
    80	chain integrity; chain-to-genesis remains `--deep`'s scope.
    81	
    82	---
    83	
    84	## D0 — The projected arrival coordinate (prerequisite for D1–D3)
    85	
    86	**Decision.** The derived index gains two columns on both `facts` and
    87	`ticks`:
    88	
    89	| column | meaning |
    90	|---|---|
    91	| `arrival_ordinal INTEGER` | the ordinal of the arrival record this row was derived from |
    92	| `arrival_seq INTEGER` | this row's 0-based position within that record's `rows_of_record` expansion |
    93	
    94	Spelled in full to match `ARRIVAL_ORDINAL_KEY = "arrival_ordinal"`
    95	(`arrival_store.py:104`) and `ResumeMark.arrival_ordinal` (`arrival.py:682`).
    96	(Open question D0-Q2 keeps the short-form alternative in front of Kyle.)
    97	
    98	`(arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq)` is the index's total order, per table.
    99	The two coordinate spaces are **per-table** in legacy stores (facts and
   100	ticks have independent rowid sequences today and no query compares a fact
   101	coordinate to a tick coordinate — windows select facts only, edges select
   102	newest-per-table) and **shared** in arrival-projected stores (the log's
   103	ordinal is one axis for all record types; density per table is not assumed
   104	anywhere).
   105	
   106	**Write sites.** Arrival-projected rows get the real coordinate at the two
   107	— and only two — sites that expand a record into rows: `arrival_store`'s
   108	catch-up indexer (`_index_record`, `:342-360`) and
   109	`arrival_projection.rederive_projections` (`:500-509`), both of which hold
   110	the record's `ResumeMark`. Legacy stores get a mirrored coordinate at
   111	append time (below). **Every other insert path is enumerated and closed**
   112	in WP-1: `SqliteStore.append`/`append_tick` (`sqlite_store.py:746`), the
   113	slice/rebirth writers in `libs/store`, and the re-sign/re-anchor UPDATE
   114	paths (`sqlite_store.py:1710-1756` — updates only, no inserts, unaffected).
   115	The shared `FACT_INSERT_SQL`/`TICK_INSERT_SQL` grow the two columns, so an
   116	insert that fails to supply them fails loudly at the statement, not
   117	silently as NULL (DP-r1-03).
   118	
   119	**Schema invariants** (DP-r1-03/DP-r2-01 — real database constraints, not
   120	statement discipline):
   121	
   122	- **New databases**: `_SCHEMA_STMTS` declares the columns with
   123	  `arrival_ordinal INTEGER NOT NULL`, `arrival_seq INTEGER NOT NULL`, and a
   124	  table-level `UNIQUE (arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq)` per table. The
   125	  invariant lives in the table, so it binds every writer including foreign
   126	  SQL, and NOT NULL moots SQLite's NULLs-pass-unique-indexes hole.
   127	- **Existing databases — table rebuild, the honest SQLite mechanism**:
   128	  retro-added columns cannot be ALTERed to NOT NULL, so the migration is
   129	  the standard rebuild, per table, in ONE transaction:
   130	  `CREATE TABLE facts_new (...full schema incl. NOT NULL + UNIQUE...)`;
   131	  `INSERT INTO facts_new SELECT ..., <coordinate>, <seq> FROM facts`
   132	  (coordinate computed per mode: `rowid, 0` mirrored, or a join against a
   133	  temp id→(ord, seq) mapping staged by the log walk for arrival-canonical);
   134	  `DROP TABLE facts; ALTER TABLE facts_new RENAME TO facts`; same for
   135	  `ticks`; recreate the table's indexes; write the `coordinate_axis`
   136	  marker; commit. No foreign keys exist on these tables, so the rebuild
   137	  needs no FK dance. **Cost, stated:** one O(store) rewrite at first
   138	  post-upgrade writable open — the same order as the coordinate walk it
   139	  absorbs (the walk and the backfill are one pass), and it happens once
   140	  per database.
   141	- The audit's NULL check (D3) remains a backstop for databases that predate
   142	  the migration or were written by a foreign schema copy — a location
   143	  claim, exactly as before.
   144	- **Legacy allocator:** for a store with no arrival log, append assigns
   145	  `arrival_ordinal = COALESCE((SELECT MAX(arrival_ordinal) FROM <table>), 0) + 1`,
   146	  `arrival_seq = 0`, inside the same transaction as the insert — one
   147	  allocator, per table, serialized by the store's existing write
   148	  transaction. For such a store the mirrored coordinate *is* its arrival
   149	  axis: dense, append-ordered, rebuild-invariant is vacuous (nothing
   150	  rebuilds it from a log), and identical to rowid order by construction.
   151	
   152	**Migration — mode-aware, not uniform** (DP-r1-02 replaced r1's option (a)):
   153	
   154	- **Legacy index (jsonl-/sqlite-canonical, no arrival log):** backfill
   155	  `arrival_ordinal = rowid, arrival_seq = 0`. Correct by construction —
   156	  rowid is that store's append order, and no batch record exists to share
   157	  an ordinal (legacy rows are row-granular).
   158	- **Arrival-canonical index:** rowid backfill is WRONG — a batch expands to
   159	  N rows sharing one ordinal (`arrival_store.py:342`), so `ordinal = rowid`
   160	  mints coordinates the log never issued and collides with the next
   161	  catch-up (the DP-r1-02 counterexample). The migration instead re-derives
   162	  coordinates **from the log**: walk `rows_of_record` over the records up
   163	  to the stamped consumed mark, matching row ids in index order and
   164	  stamping `(ord, seq)`; any mismatch between walk and index refuses into
   165	  the existing escape hatch — `rederive_projections`, which rebuilds the
   166	  index with coordinates written natively. The walk is O(store), once.
   167	- **Migration dispatch — a common upgrader on every writable open**
   168	  (DP-r2-02: `_ensure_index_schema` sits on the projection-rederive path
   169	  only, `arrival_projection.py:479/:544`, while the shared
   170	  `FACT_INSERT_SQL`/`TICK_INSERT_SQL` change hits ordinary `SqliteStore`
   171	  and `JsonlStore` writers immediately, `sqlite_store.py:131`,
   172	  `jsonl_store.py:668`). One module-level function beside the schema
   173	  statements it extends — `ensure_coordinate_schema(conn, mode)` in
   174	  `sqlite_store.py` — invoked before any insert SQL runs, by every writer
   175	  of these tables, following the store's existing lazy-migration idiom
   176	  (`_ensure_chain_columns`/`_ensure_fact_signature_column`,
   177	  `sqlite_store.py:1495-1520`, already called from the write entry
   178	  points):
   179	  1. `SqliteStore` — from the same pre-write hooks that run the two
   180	     existing `_ensure_*` migrations, with `mode="mirrored"`;
   181	  2. `JsonlStore` — same hook pattern on its index connection,
   182	     `mode="mirrored"` (its index rows are row-granular append-order);
   183	  3. `ArrivalStore` — at open, `mode="arrival"`;
   184	  4. `arrival_projection._ensure_index_schema` — delegates to it,
   185	     `mode="arrival"`.
   186	  The mode is passed explicitly by the constructor that knows what it is;
   187	  where a connection is opened on an existing file without that knowledge,
   188	  it is derived authoritatively from the store's own markers — the
   189	  `ARRIVAL_LINEAGE_KEY` resume mark in `store_meta` means arrival, its
   190	  absence means mirrored — never guessed from file suffix.
   191	- **Interrupted migration:** the rebuild is one transaction per table and
   192	  the `store_meta` marker (`coordinate_axis = mirrored | arrival`) commits
   193	  inside the second table's transaction. Marker present ⇒ migrated, the
   194	  upgrader no-ops; absent ⇒ the rebuild runs (idempotently — a re-run of a
   195	  completed first table is a no-op rebuild). A crash between the two table
   196	  transactions leaves no half state a reader can observe: the marker is
   197	  absent, so the next writable open re-runs the whole upgrader. There is
   198	  no observable half-migrated state.
   199	
   200	**Scope-the-claim corollary.** `arrival_ordinal IS NULL` in a migrated
   201	index is evidence of an out-of-band insert. The check is spelled "this
   202	index holds a row carrying no arrival coordinate" — a location claim,
   203	never "this store is clean" — and after DP-r1-03's statement-level closure
   204	it should never fire except on genuinely foreign writes, which is what an
   205	audit backstop is for.
   206	
   207	---
   208	
   209	## D1 — Witness re-key
   210	
   211	**The new data model** (`witness.py:164-195`):
   212	
   213	```python
   214	@dataclass(frozen=True)
   215	class WitnessPosition:
   216	    fact_id: str              # unchanged — the durable handle
   217	    arrival_lineage: str | None  # NEW — the log whose ordinal axis this indexes;
   218	                              # None = no arrival axis (legacy store / storeless bootstrap)
   219	    ordinal: int              # REPLACES rowid — the arrival record cutoff; -1 = empty prefix
   220	    seq: int                  # unchanged meaning — count of rows at-or-before
   221	    lineage: str | None       # unchanged — the store's own_lineage (declaration lineage)
   222	    unadopted: bool           # unchanged
   223	    anchor: TickAnchor | None # unchanged
   224	    store: str                # unchanged
   225	```
   226	
   227	`lineage` and `arrival_lineage` are **different identities and both stay**:
   228	`lineage` is what A10 qualifies a portable handle by; `arrival_lineage`
   229	names the coordinate axis. r2 change (DP-r1-09): the no-axis state is
   230	`None`, not `""` — r1's empty-string spelling conflated "legacy store-local
   231	axis" with "storeless bootstrap", and `None` makes axis-absence explicit
   232	and unequal to every real lineage. The same-path replacement guard (below)
   233	fires only when **both** sides carry a real axis.
   234	
   235	**Empty-prefix sentinel.** Today `rowid = 0`; arrival ordinals start at 0
   236	(genesis), so the empty prefix is `ordinal = -1`. Genesis projects no rows,
   237	so the selected set is unchanged; `-1` is the honest spelling.
   238	`handle.py:852-855`'s storeless bootstrap moves to
   239	`ordinal=-1, arrival_lineage=None`.
   240	
   241	**Selection.** Every `rowid <= ?` cutoff becomes `arrival_ordinal <= ?`
   242	(record-granular arm; see D1-Q1 for the composite alternative). Enumerated
   243	sites, all libs/engine: `store_reader.facts_between` (`:631`),
   244	`facts_by_kind` (`:677`), `query_facts` pagination (`:809-816`),
   245	`declaration._decl_lineage_and_head_on_conn` (`:654-656`),
   246	`witness._resolve_witness_position_on_conn` seq count (`:317`),
   247	`witness.diff_interval_report` (`:624-641`), `witness._resolve_anchor`.
   248	The fold-replay and event-cursor prefix queries
   249	(`sqlite_store.py:1203-1292`, `:1359`, `:1402`, `:2018-2069` — "FOLD REPLAY
   250	ORDER is receipt order (rowid)") re-key to
   251	`ORDER BY arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq` in the same package: they are the
   252	ordering-authority read path the coordinate exists for. `at_rowid` →
   253	`at_ordinal` rides along; leaving it is forbidden residue.
   254	
   255	**The app-side rowid consumer** (DP-r1-07 — r1's "all sites inside
   256	libs/engine" was false). `fold.py:1525-1536` picks the diff baseline by
   257	comparing `pos1.rowid`/`pos2.rowid`. Two designs, Kyle rules (merged into
   258	open question D-Q2 with the audit dispatch, since both are apps/ touches):
   259	
   260	- **(i) Engine primitive (recommended).** `diff_interval_report` already
   261	  computes the baseline; it gains a `baseline` field naming which input
   262	  position is the lower endpoint. The app reads the field instead of
   263	  comparing coordinates — the knowledge moves to the layer that owns it
   264	  (the comment at `fold.py:1529-1536` documents that only attribution, not
   265	  computation, belongs to the app). App diff: a few lines in one function.
   266	- (ii) A public ordering predicate (`WitnessPosition.precedes(other)`, same
   267	  refusals as `diff_interval_report`) — keeps the comparison app-side but
   268	  re-keyed. Still touches apps/.
   269	
   270	**Serialization / compat.** `durable_handle` unchanged, verbatim. No
   271	migration.
   272	
   273	**Same-lineage re-resolution** (`verify_position_for_store:466-516`) keeps
   274	its exact structure and both refusal branches **byte-identical, messages
   275	included** (DP-r1-09 tightened this): the new same-path guard — target is
   276	arrival-canonical, position carries a real `arrival_lineage`, and it does
   277	not match the target log's lineage (a store file replaced in place under
   278	the same path) — raises a **separately-typed refusal**
