   508	## Gate plan
   509	
   510	Independent gate agent; re-derives the oracle from scratch; pointer branch
   511	`slice/D-gate`. (r2: gates for every DP-r1 gap; the impossible and
   512	non-discriminating r1 gates replaced — DP-r1-10.)
   513	
   514	**G-D0-1 — the shuffled index (the ruled gate item).** A natural rebuild
   515	cannot produce one (`rederive_projections` replays in order), so the gate
   516	builds a **deliberate permuted-insert harness** — index rows inserted in
   517	permuted record order so rowid order and arrival order disagree.
   518	Assertions: L1 passes; every witness position, seal window, audit check,
   519	and `verify_chain` answer identical to the unshuffled store. Written first;
   520	used by every later package.
   521	**G-D0-2** — deliberately rebuilt index: outstanding positions and seals
   522	valid.
   523	**G-D0-3** — migration of an existing **batch-bearing arrival-canonical**
   524	index: coordinates equal the log's (the DP-r1-02 counterexample as a test);
   525	subsequent catch-up appends collide with nothing.
   526	**G-D0-4** — interrupted migration: kill between schema-add and stamp;
   527	reopen; the store answers correctly and the marker semantics hold (no
   528	observable half-state).
   529	**G-D0-5** — invariant enforcement **by the table**: on a migrated (rebuilt)
   530	and on a freshly-created database, a raw `conn.execute` INSERT with a
   531	duplicate `(arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq)` raises `IntegrityError`
   532	(UNIQUE), and one with NULL in either coordinate raises `IntegrityError`
   533	(NOT NULL) — foreign SQL included, no store-layer code in the loop; legacy
   534	allocator monotonic under interleaved fact/tick appends in one transaction.
   535	**G-D0-6** — ordinary legacy opens migrate (the DP-r1-10 re-fail gap):
   536	existing sqlite-canonical and jsonl-canonical fixture databases opened
   537	through their normal public constructors and appended to via the public
   538	API — the upgrader ran before the first insert, coordinates are correct
   539	and constrained, prior rows carry the mirror backfill, and a read-only
   540	open of an unmigrated database still answers reads.
   541	
   542	**G-D1-2** — `WitnessAggregateUnsupported` at all three sites,
   543	messages unchanged; A10 refusal messages byte-identical. Pinning tests
   544	(`test_witness_position.py`, `test_diff_interval_report.py`,
   545	`test_query_facts.py`, `test_fold_at.py`) pass untouched.
   546	**G-D1-3** — `seq:N` round-trip across a batch boundary, pinning the ruled
   547	D1-Q1 arm.
   548	**G-D1-4** — same-path store-replacement: new typed refusal fires;
   549	A10 and the unadopted refusal unchanged (their tests prove it).
   550	**G-D1-5** — position equivalence (replaces r1's non-discriminating
   551	G-D1-1): for a corpus store, every fact's resolved position selects the
   552	same prefix row-set before and after the re-key; `durable_handle` output
   553	unchanged as a corollary.
   554	**G-D1-6** — fold diff baseline: reversed `--diff B..A` attributes the
   555	baseline identically pre/post re-key (whichever D-Q2 design is ruled).
   556	
   557	**G-D2-1** — pre-D sealed fixture: `verify_chain` green AND each window
   558	hash byte-equal to the legacy computation.
   559	**G-D2-2** — a seal spanning a batch record on a batch-bearing store:
   560	window membership and hash identical before/after migration.
   561	**G-D2-3** — unresolvable cursor still hashes as empty.
   562	**G-D2-4** — verification under permutation: `verify_chain`'s window
   563	counts and verdicts identical on the shuffled index (the DP-r1-04
   564	verification-side residue, gated).
   565	**G-D2-5** — rowid ratchet: no ORDER BY / range / COUNT on rowid in
